WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.370
<v Stephane>Okay, so a quick lecture on SQS.</v>

2
00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.410
So as we know, SQS is serverless.

3
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.280
It's a managed queue,

4
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.100
and it's fully integrated with IAM for security.

5
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.200
And the beauty is that it has extreme scale

6
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.550
for SQS Standard.

7
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.710
You don't need to provision anything.

8
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:18.000
The main purpose to SQS is to decouple between your services

9
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.040
to be able to scale them independently

10
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.650
and perform some work asynchronously.

11
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.480
SQS does not handle big messages.

12
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.480
The max message size is 1,024 kilobytes.

13
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.100
So in case you want to send

14
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.070
maybe a 10-megabyte file through SQS,

15
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.640
you would instead upload that file into Amazon S3

16
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.940
and then retrieve the S3 key for your object

17
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.790
and send that through SQS instead.

18
00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.850
And then your consumer would retrieve the key

19
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.160
and then retrieve the message from S3,

20
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.200
and this would work just fine.

21
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.480
So all the messages from SQS queues

22
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.880
can be read from EC2 instances,

23
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:54.930
and they could be managed

24
00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.670
by an Auto Scaling group,

25
00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.200
or it could be an AWS Lambda function using an event mapper.

26
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.020
Another common architecture with SQS is to use it

27
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.420
as a write buffer for DynamoDB.

28
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.420
So instead of directly writing to DynamoDB

29
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.460
and maybe getting some throttling issue,

30
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.620
you would first write to SQS,

31
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.380
and it could scale seamlessly, so you're fine.

32
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.990
And then you would have an application dedicated

33
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.540
to take these writes from SQS

34
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.120
and write them into DynamoDB.

35
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.030
Finally, there's SQS FIFO for First In and First Out.

36
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.490
It means that the messages are received

37
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.860
and read in the order they were sent.

38
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.150
And then because there is

39
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.400
this ordering constraint with FIFO,

40
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.790
then you get 300 messages per second without batching

41
00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.400
or up to 3,000 messages per second with batching,

42
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.650
so there is limited scale here.

43
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.220
Now let's talk about dead-letter queues in SQS.

44
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.140
The idea is that let's get into the scenario

45
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.780
where a consumer fails to process a message

46
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.540
within the Visibility Timeout period.

47
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.010
Then we know that the message

48
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.260
automatically goes back into the queue.

49
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.520
So the consumer reads the message.

50
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.720
Maybe there's a failure.

51
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.220
Maybe we don't have enough time.

52
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.500
The message goes back into the queue.

53
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.440
Now if that thing happens very often,

54
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.370
that can be a problem.

55
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.080
For example, we read again the message,

56
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.700
maybe there's something wrong with the message.

57
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.440
Maybe a consumer doesn't understand the message

58
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.180
or can't process the message.

59
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.220
Then the message will go back into the queue,

60
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.150
and it will happen again.

61
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.040
We'll read the message again from SQS,

62
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.740
and it will go again back into the queue.

63
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.430
So we can set a threshold to how many times this can happen.

64
00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.680
And this failure loop can be a big problem,

65
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.520
but we can set a MaximumReceives threshold.

66
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.950
And if that threshold is exceeded,

67
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.727
then we can tell SQS to say,

68
00:02:36.727 --> 00:02:38.640
"Well, this message looks a bit odd.

69
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.100
It looks like it's being processed too many times

70
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.660
and not successfully.

71
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.770
Therefore, send this into a dead-letter queue."

72
00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.359
And the dead-letter queue will contain that message

73
00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:50.850
for later processing,

74
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.460
and so the message will be removed from the first queue

75
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.320
and sent into the second one.

76
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.330
Why do we have dead-letter queues?

77
00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.660
Well, dead-letter queues are very useful for debugging,

78
00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.510
and in case a message goes in a dead-letter queue,

79
00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.337
because it is an SQS, you have to process it,

80
00:03:05.337 --> 00:03:06.600
but at least it gives you time

81
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.910
to understand what is happening.

82
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:09.743
A few things to notice

83
00:03:09.743 --> 00:03:11.820
that the dead-letter queue of FIFO queue

84
00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.190
must also be a FIFO queue,

85
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.830
and the dead-letter queue of the Standard queue

86
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.590
must also be a Standard queue.

87
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.390
Finally, because we have a dead-letter queue,

88
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.660
you need to make sure that the messages are processed

89
00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.700
before they expire from the queue.

90
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.380
So it's a good idea to set a long time,

91
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.570
for example, 14 days of retention,

92
00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.343
from within the dead-letter queue.

93
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.140
And the next feature

94
00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.450
for management of your dead-letter queues

95
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.150
are the Redrive to Source feature.

96
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.640
So it's a feature to help you consume messages

97
00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.410
in the dead-letter queue

98
00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.300
to understand what is wrong with them.

99
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.010
So you have your messages now.

100
00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.720
You know they haven't been processed

101
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.950
in the source queue,

102
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.440
and therefore, they are in the dead-letter queue,

103
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.810
and you're going to do a manual inspection

104
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.180
and debugging of these messages.

105
00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.700
And then you're going to fix your consumer code,

106
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.460
understand why the message wasn't processed

107
00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.100
in case the message was correct,

108
00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.410
and then what you can do is redrive

109
00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.780
that message from the dead-letter queue

110
00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.960
into the source SQS queue.

111
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.730
And what's going to happen with this

112
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.360
is that the consumer can now reprocess that message

113
00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.680
without even knowing

114
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.050
that the message went into the dead-letter queue,

115
00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.660
and then the message processing has happened.

116
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.100
And that's a cool feature.

117
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.990
In terms of idempotency,

118
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.970
because messages can be processed twice

119
00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.710
by consumers in case there is a failure,

120
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.210
a timeout, or something like this,

121
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.770
you need to make sure that your consumers are idempotent.

122
00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.180
And what does idempotent mean?

123
00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.820
That means that if you process the same message twice,

124
00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.770
if you do the same action twice,

125
00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.890
then the effect will not be duplicated.

126
00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:48.690
So we'll show you an example right now.

127
00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.970
We are pulling messages from SQS,

128
00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.410
and we're doing long polling

129
00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:56.490
in order to minimize cost and minimize latency.

130
00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.570
And our EC2 consumer is writing into DynamoDB.

131
00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.040
After writing into DynamoDB,

132
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.300
our EC2 consumer will delete the message from SQS

133
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.190
so that it's not processed by another consumer.

134
00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.980
But in case something goes wrong,

135
00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.490
then your EC2 consumer will do two writes into DynamoDB

136
00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.770
because it will read the same message twice.

137
00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.170
So if you're inserting into DynamoDB

138
00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.510
a new row anytime you read from SQS,

139
00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:22.680
it's not an idempotent

140
00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.050
because if you read twice the same message,

141
00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.590
then you will insert two rows into DynamoDB.

142
00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:29.703
But if you're doing upsert

143
00:05:29.703 --> 00:05:32.880
into DynamoDB using a primary key, for example,

144
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.970
then in case the message already exists in DynamoDB,

145
00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.490
it's just going to be overwritten or updated,

146
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:40.590
and therefore, this is an idempotent request

147
00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.100
because there will only be one row at the end in DynamoDB,

148
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.590
even if you read that message twice.

149
00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.500
So just remember, idempotency is very important

150
00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.240
when writing an EC2 consumer

151
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.390
or any kind of consumer on top of SQS.

152
00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.900
Okay, now we also see that Lambda can read from SQS

153
00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.910
and using an event source mapping.

154
00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.150
And so the event source mapper will pull from SQS

155
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:03.983
and return a batch

156
00:06:03.983 --> 00:06:06.210
and then invoke a Lambda function with that batch.

157
00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:07.950
And so the way the event mapper works

158
00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.140
is that it, by default, uses long polling,

159
00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.000
so it's optimized in that regards.

160
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.410
We specify a batch size,

161
00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.780
so between one to 10 messages.

162
00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:16.920
If we do one messages,

163
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.430
then we are reading as fast as we can message by message.

164
00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:23.130
If we're doing 10, we are going to be more efficient

165
00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.380
and then we have to configure it.

166
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.940
So this is very simple.

167
00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.207
The event mapper says,

168
00:06:28.207 --> 00:06:30.540
"Which SQS queue we are reading from,

169
00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.760
and what is the batch size going to be?"

170
00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.010
Okay, the recommended value, though,

171
00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:36.270
for the Queue Visibility Timeout

172
00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.570
is to be 6x the timeout of your Lambda function.

173
00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:42.390
And that is from recommendation from the documentation.

174
00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.350
The idea is that

175
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.750
if you are reading the same message multiple times,

176
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:46.800
you want to make sure

177
00:06:47.855 --> 00:06:50.190
it doesn't time out in your Lambda function,.

178
00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.800
Okay, to use a dead-letter queue,

179
00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.400
you can set it up at the SQS queue level, not in Lambda.

180
00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.230
Remember, the DLQ for Lambda

181
00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.020
is only when you have asynchronous invocation.

182
00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.370
So in case you want messages

183
00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.970
that you can't process to be removed from your queue,

184
00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.790
then you need to set up a DLQ on the SQS queue itself,

185
00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:09.870
not the Lambda function.

186
00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.040
This will not work.

187
00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:12.930
Or we've seen there's a newer feature

188
00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:14.970
on Lambda called Lambda Destinations,

189
00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.860
in which case, using Lambda Destinations,

190
00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:18.900
we can have the Lambda function itself

191
00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.100
throw away these events

192
00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.620
that don't work for us into a destination,

193
00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.570
and the destination can be an SNS topic

194
00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.790
or an SQS queue, for example.

195
00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.970
Okay, one last Solution Architecture with SQS.

196
00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:30.960
We have our clients,

197
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.880
and he wants to do a lot of work,

198
00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.590
but we're going to decouple things.

199
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.000
So we're going to put these things

200
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.310
into an SQS request queue,

201
00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.410
and there's going to be a work processor

202
00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.450
reading from that queue and doing the work.

203
00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.290
When it's done,

204
00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.800
it's going to send a message to an SQS response queue,

205
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.710
and the clients will be reading from that queue.

206
00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.540
Why would we have such architecture?

207
00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:52.650
Well, we get full decoupling,

208
00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:54.990
so we can scale the left-hand side

209
00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.940
and the right-hand side independently.

210
00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.080
There's fault tolerance,

211
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.690
so in case something fails in our work processor,

212
00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.880
another work processor will pick it up.

213
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.170
And then we have load balancing,

214
00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:05.910
so we're able to scale horizontally

215
00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.800
in the way we are handling requests.

216
00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.210
And on top of this,

217
00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:10.650
SQS itself scales really well,

218
00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.000
so this is good.

219
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.560
Just an idea of architecture,

220
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.970
but again, SQS is really use it as you want.

221
00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.070
There's so many different architectures at SQS.

222
00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:21.510
It's really up to finding the question,

223
00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.430
but anytime you see some decoupling

224
00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.620
and making sure that the work can be processed

225
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:26.460
at least once,

226
00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.830
then SQS is going to be a great answer.

227
00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.090
All right, that's it for this lecture.

228
00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.840
I will see you in the next lecture.

